  "worked_solution": "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in miles per hour, we divide the number of miles, which is $20,$ by the number of hours, $4.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{4} & =20\u00f74 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the marathon runner is $5$ miles per hour. \n\nTo find the time it takes the marathon runner to cover $26$ miles, we divide the distance by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5} \\\\  & =26\u00f75 \\\\  & =5.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the marathon runner $5.2$ hours to run $26$ miles.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
